    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the full test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\n`locality` is a read-time derived field, so I followed the same pattern the codebase already uses for `displayName`, `initials`, and `membershipTier`:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `readOnly` `locality` property to the `Owner` response schema (this generates `OwnerDto.locality` with its getter/setter at build time).\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 A small helper with `static String derive(String city)` implementing the fixed table: `Sydney\u2192NSW`, `Melbourne\u2192VIC`, `Brisbane\u2192QLD`, and `UNKNOWN` for anything else (including null).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", expression = \"java(OwnerLocality.derive(owner.getCity()))\")` on `toOwnerDto`, with `imports = OwnerLocality.class` on the `@Mapper` annotation.\n\n## A trap worth flagging\n\nMy first attempt put `deriveLocality` directly on the `OwnerMapper` interface. That made **every existing test fail with a 500** \u2014 because MapStruct treats any single-argument `String\u2192String` method on a mapper as an *implicit conversion* and silently applied it to all String fields, breaking telephone E.164 normalization on `POST /api/owners`. Moving the helper to a separate class fixed it. (I diagnosed this by reverting to baseline and confirming the base repo was green on a clean build.)\n\n## Test results\n\n`./mvnw -o clean test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the repo's only test class, `AcceptanceTest`, covering both the known-city `NSW` case and the unknown-city `UNKNOWN` case).\n\nOne note: builds behave differently clean vs. incremental here (stale generated MapStruct/OpenAPI sources can mask failures), so results are only trustworthy after `clean`.",
